{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/351133-ways-to-boost-mental-resistance","id":351133,"title":"Ways to boost mental resistance?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-12-26T18:21:38-08:00","id":351133,"title":"Ways to boost mental resistance?","content":"Lately my Hunter group has been facing more powerful threats, many of which have mind-influencing powers such as vampires and powerful mages. Sometimes the group does ok, but against mental influencing powers, they usually end up being overwhelmed. My concern is that it feels like even characters that are fairly resilient, and have taken merits like indomitable and favored weapon, along with tactics like Moral Support, still fold pretty easily against even basic vampire powers from V:TR 2.0 and Mages.<br /> <br /> I was wondering if there were other merits or tricks floating around I hadn't considered.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/819-vaneheart","name":"Vaneheart","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar819_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-26T19:40:07-08:00","id":351187,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure if you can brute force this problem. Between moral support, indomitable, and favored weapon you're in better shape to resist mind influencing effects than many supernatural pcs. What endowments do your group have access to?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3755-jaerys","name":"Jaerys","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"127"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-26T21:19:34-08:00","id":351297,"title":"","content":"The answer has two parts, and part one is just that mortal hunters are at a big disadvantage when facing mental influence powers, and their best means of dealing with them once they know they're in play is to carefully avoid placing themselves in situations where they might be targeted. Trash the witch's sanctum while he's out retrieving some artifact; don't confront the vampire while it's walking around during the night; call in false police alarms or hire gang strikes to attack without showing up in person. This is really hard to do, yes. The hunters are at a big disadvantage.<br /> <br /> Part two is that the powers in Vampire are written under the primary assumption that they will be used in Vampire games starring vampires, and when you're spending blood on your disciplines it is not a very interesting result when nothing happens, so yes, a Vampire power that's activated has a good chance of succeeding. They'll work in Hunter games, but they're not written for Hunter games, so the results won't play out optimally for Hunter, which is one of the reasons there are those Dread Powers scattered around. In the same spirit, whether you use the Dread Powers or the Disciplines and Arcana, consider tweaking them with some added limitations (requirements like Dominate's eye contact, restricted spheres of influence or some means or trigger by which the victim can actively attempt to throw off the influence rather than passively resist). The Achilles heel to a monster's given power is a frequent genre staple for hunter stories.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":522,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","user":"Stupid Loserman","date":". 12-27-2014, 12:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-26T22:15:42-08:00","id":351349,"title":"","content":"I'm of the view that Hunters are <i>supposed </i>to have issues dealing with such powers: a monster that can rip your arms off, while threatening, is not nearly as frightening as one that can literally rewrite your identity, even for a short period. So, if your players are having trouble dealing with that, that's really the system working as intended. So the players choices are, as Stupid Loserman mentioned, try to work around ever being targeted with them.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, of course, there's the Golden Rule. In that spirit, maybe allow them to spend a Willpower to throw off the effect for a short time (a turn is common) in appropriate circumstances, such as the controller issuing orders that go against one of the Hunters core beliefs (Virtue/Vice, Aspirations or breaking points)? Or come up with either a new Merit, or alter Indomitable, to give a -1 penalty per dot (changing it to a 1 - 5 Merit rather than a flat 2): I like the idea of coming up with a new Merit that represents your characters insane dedication to a particular idea, which gives them a bonus on resisting attempts to sway them against that belief. One thing to bear in mind is that resisting such tricks should never be <i>easy</i>, or at least not easier than it is for the monster to do the effect. If you could buy up your resistance to such things for, say, 1 XP per dice, but the power costs say 2XP per dice, then you're taking the fangs out of the power, both mechanically and thematically.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-27T14:01:03-08:00","id":351945,"title":"","content":"Honestly, this seems like more of an issue with mental influencing powers than with hunters specifically. If the hunters are using favored weapons, indomitable, and a successful application of moral support they're rolling at least composure/resolve + 5. That's comparable to most &quot;mid level&quot; supernatural creatures. The OP mentioned mages, so I will point out that there are protective amulets in the GMC equipment list that can add another 2 dice to contest harmful spells. They can overload if the mage is getting exceptional successes though. How likely that is depends on whether they're using rotes or improvised spell, though composure/resolve+5 would probably be good enough to resist most improvised spells that weren't cast as a ritual.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3755-jaerys","name":"Jaerys","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"127"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}